Step 1 of 3 of the owner's decision on canlin#112 and #114 (the model view gets its own canlin_ui_view). The shell's pane holds one view per module today: layout_pane (src/shell/shell.c:370-398) computes one rect, reserves the model label strip above it, and draw_pane (:1258-1286) fills that rect and draws the strip with the text 'Model-generated view'. The shell knows exactly one bit per module - sh->mods[k].model - and it means 'this module's pane is a model pane'. Give the shell the ability to hold a SECOND view for that module: the model's own view, with its own rectangle, its own frame, the label strip belonging to IT rather than to the module's view, and the model's rectangle rendered. The module's own widgets then sit outside the frame, which is what UI §4 describes. The shell must also keep the input routing right: the input goes to the view under the point, and a click in the model's area must reach the model's widgets and not the module's. Spec sentences this touches: shell §1 ('One view per module instance') and §3 (the model-view frame bullet), UI §2 ('one vertical scroll region') and §4. Write the spec sentences as the change lands, or say in your report what they must say. Depends on: the app that creates the model's view (canlin#129) and the binding that draws into it (canlin#130) - the shell's part lands first and is exercised by its own tests.

